# Block art embedded via go:embed and its markdown source must stay LF byte-for-byte on
# every platform — a CRLF checkout would bake \r into the binary and break TUI rendering.
internal/tui/logo.txt text eol=lf
graphics/apogee-logo.md text eol=lf

# Every golden and testdata file is compared byte-for-byte by tuitest.compareGolden /
# GoldenText, so it must be LF on every platform — a core.autocrlf=true checkout (Windows CI)
# would otherwise turn a byte-identical frame into a diff that looks identical.
*.golden text eol=lf
**/testdata/** text eol=lf

# ...except binary fixtures, which the line above would otherwise mark as text: a CRLF byte pair
# inside a PDF or an image would be rewritten to LF on `git add` and corrupt the fixture.
**/testdata/**/*.pdf binary
**/testdata/**/*.png binary
**/testdata/**/*.gif binary
**/testdata/**/*.jpg binary

# The go:embed'd defaults must stay LF for the same reason the block art above does: //go:embed
# bakes the bytes into the binary verbatim, so a core.autocrlf=true checkout ships a daemon unit,
# a plist and a starter config whose every line ends in a carriage return, and which `apogee
# daemon install` then writes out that way. It also fails the generation tests, which compare a
# rendering against an LF .golden and so report a diff that prints identically. VERSION is
# embedded too — strings.TrimSpace in version.go already saves the version NUMBER, but it is
# pinned here so no embedded file is left relying on its reader to trim.
cmd/apogee/defaults/** text eol=lf
internal/config/defaults/** text eol=lf
VERSION text eol=lf

# Git hooks are POSIX sh scripts run by Git Bash on Windows: a core.autocrlf=true checkout
# would end every line in a carriage return and break them, so they stay LF everywhere.
.beads/hooks/** text eol=lf

# The release scripts run under bash, Git Bash included: a core.autocrlf=true checkout would end
# every line in a carriage return and break them, so they stay LF everywhere.
scripts/*.sh text eol=lf
